Here is a clear, interview‑ready comparison of For Each, For Each (Parallel), and Scatter‑Gather in MuleSoft — with when to use what, differences, and examples.

✅ 1. For Each (Sequential)
What it does
Processes items in a collection one by one, in order, sequentially.

✅ Each item is processed one at a time
✅ Order is preserved
✅ Easy to use and predictable
✅ Good for small or medium collections

Example
If payload = [1,2,3], Mule will process:

1 → then 2 → then 3
Best for
Sequential transformations
Sending multiple emails
Inserting multiple DB rows one by one
Processing a small JSON array
Limitations
❌ Slow for large lists
❌ Cannot run in parallel

✅ 2. For Each (Parallel For Each)
What it does
Processes items in parallel, meaning multiple items are processed at the same time.

✅ Great for improving performance
✅ Runs each item in different threads
✅ Faster for large lists or slow external calls

BUT:
❌ Order is NOT guaranteed
❌ Heavy parallelism can overload DB or API
❌ Must be used carefully

Example
If payload = [1,2,3,4], parallel for-each may process:

3 and 1 at same time → then 4 → then 2
Best for
Calling APIs in parallel
Large payloads
Performance-sensitive flows
Independent operations
Config Options
Max concurrency → max parallel threads
Timeout
✅ 3. Scatter-Gather
What it does
Executes multiple different flows in parallel and combines all responses into one.

✅ Used for calling multiple systems at the same time
✅ Each route is independent
✅ Aggregates responses into a single payload

Example diagram
         ┌── Call ERP
Request ─┤
         ├── Call SAP
         ├── Call Salesforce
         └── Call Database
All run in parallel, and Mule returns a combined output.

Best for
Parallel calls to different APIs
Aggregation patterns
Multi-system lookups
Real-time orchestration
Limitations
❌ Not for looping over a list
❌ Not for 100+ parallel calls (better use Parallel For Each)

✅ Key Differences at a Glance
Feature	For Each	Parallel For Each	Scatter-Gather
Purpose	Loop items one by one	Loop items in parallel	Call multiple routes in parallel
Parallel?	❌ No	✅ Yes	✅ Yes
Input	Collection (array/list)	Collection (array/list)	Multiple flows/routes
Order maintained?	✅ Yes	❌ No	✅ Aggregated
Best for	Sequential loops	Speeding up loops	Parallel system calls
Output	Processed list	Processed list	Array of outputs from all routes
✅ How to Remember Easily
✅ For Each → “process one by one”
✅ Parallel For Each → “process list items parallelly”
✅ Scatter-Gather → “call different systems parallelly and combine results”

✅ Examples of When to Use What
✅ Use For Each
Insert 50 records into DB maintaining order.

✅ Use Parallel For Each
Call 50 APIs simultaneously to speed up response time.

✅ Use Scatter-Gather
Call SAP + Salesforce + DB at the same time and merge responses.

