✅ What is MUnit in MuleSoft?
MUnit is MuleSoft’s testing framework designed specifically for Mule applications.
It is used to create automated unit tests, integration tests, mock external systems, and validate flow logic.

Think of MUnit as JUnit for MuleSoft.

✅ Why MUnit is Important?
✅ Ensures Mule flows work correctly
✅ Catches bugs early
✅ Helps maintain code quality
✅ Required in CI/CD pipelines
✅ Validates DataWeave transformations
✅ Supports mocking of connectors
✅ Required for production-grade delivery

✅ Main Features of MUnit
✅ 1. Unit Testing Mule Flows
Run individual flows and verify if they function correctly.

✅ 2. Mocking
You can mock:

HTTP requests
DB connectors
Salesforce operations
Any external system
So your tests do not depend on real systems.

✅ 3. Assertions
Check whether:

payload is expected
variables have correct values
attributes are correct
error is thrown when expected
✅ 4. Coverage Reports
MUnit provides code coverage for:

Flows
Subflows
Routers
Minimum coverage is often enforced in organizations (e.g., 80%).

✅ 5. Error Testing
MUnit can validate on-error-continue and on-error-propagate.

✅ MUnit Test Structure
Every MUnit test consists of 2 major sections:

✅ Before/After Test
✅ Test Suite containing test cases

Example structure:

Test Suite
 ├── Before Test
 ├── Test Case 1
 ├── Test Case 2
 └── After Test
✅ MUnit Test Case Structure
A test case includes:

✅ 1. Execution
Where the Mule flow is executed:




XML
<munit:execution>
    <flow-ref name="myFlow"/>
</munit:execution>
✅ 2. Mocking
Mock connectors to avoid calling real systems:




XML
<munit-tools:mock-when processor="db:select">
    <munit-tools:then-return payload='#[{"status":"ok"}]'/>
</munit-tools:mock-when>
✅ 3. Assertions
Check expected values:




XML
<munit-tools:assert-that 
    expression="#[payload.status]" 
    is="#[MunitTools::equalTo('ok')]"/>
✅ Important Components of MUnit
✅ 1. Mock When
Used to simulate connector behavior.

✅ 2. Spy
Used to inspect how many times a processor was executed.

✅ 3. Assert That
Validate variables, payload, attributes.

✅ 4. Assert Equals
Simple value comparison.

✅ 5. Verify Call
Validate that a connector was called:




XML
<munit-tools:verify-call processor="http:request" times="1"/>
✅ Real-Time Examples
✅ 1. Mocking an HTTP Call
Instead of calling a real API:




XML
<munit-tools:mock-when processor="http:request">
    <munit-tools:with-attributes>
        <munit-tools:equal-to name="method" value="GET"/>
    </munit-tools:with-attributes>
    <munit-tools:then-return payload="#[{result:'success'}]"/>
</munit-tools:mock-when>
✅ 2. Testing DB Insert Flow
Mock DB to avoid inserting into real database:




XML
<munit-tools:mock-when processor="db:insert">
    <munit-tools:then-return payload="#[{updateCount:1}]"/>
</munit-tools:mock-when>
✅ 3. Error Testing
Check if a flow throws expected error:




XML
<munit-tools:expect-error 
    type="MULE:CONNECTIVITY" 
    when="myFlow"/>
✅ MUnit Coverage
MuleSoft counts coverage for:

Flow references
Choice routers
Set payload
Transform Message
Error handlers
Minimum recommended coverage in real projects: ✅ 80% or above

✅ MUnit Best Practices
✅ Mock ALL external systems (DB, HTTP, Salesforce, SFTP)
✅ Keep tests independent (no reliance on previous tests)
✅ Validate payload + vars + attributes
✅ Use meaningful assertion messages
✅ Test both positive and negative scenarios
✅ Use test resources (sample input JSON/XML)
✅ Automate MUnit in CI/CD

✅ Short Interview Explanation
MUnit is MuleSoft’s testing framework that allows developers to test Mule flows, mock external systems, validate payloads, and generate coverage. It supports assertions, mocks, spies, and error handling to ensure application quality before deployment.

